        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "html": "By this time, LTSC had formed a collaborative partnership for the old church project with a community visual arts organization called “Visual Communications” (VC).  VC over the years had been renting various locations in the Little Tokyo area for their operations but they wanted to have a permanent home base for their visual arts programs.  VC was led by an energetic Executive Director named Linda Mabalot, who was a kindred spirit in wanting to create a space for the arts in the community and she was a great partner for this development project.  Since EWP had decided earlier not to move into the church, we allotted a large prime space for VC in the building’s basement which had served as the former church’s social hall.  A little bit later, local artist and art instructor Dr. Lydia Takeshita, the founder of LA Artcore, requested a space for an art gallery, which we agreed to provide in the space formerly used for Sunday School classes on the first floor.",
          "text": "By this time, LTSC had formed a collaborative partnership for the old church project with a community visual arts organization called “Visual Communications” (VC).  VC over the years had been renting various locations in the Little Tokyo area for their operations but they wanted to have a permanent home base for their visual arts programs.  VC was led by an energetic Executive Director named Linda Mabalot, who was a kindred spirit in wanting to create a space for the arts in the community and she was a great partner for this development project.  Since EWP had decided earlier not to move into the church, we allotted a large prime space for VC in the building’s basement which had served as the former church’s social hall.  A little bit later, local artist and art instructor Dr. Lydia Takeshita, the founder of LA Artcore, requested a space for an art gallery, which we agreed to provide in the space formerly used for Sunday School classes on the first floor."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "html": "Meanwhile, EWP, after searching various sites, was not able to secure an alternate location for a theater in Hollywood.  I suggested to them to re-consider moving into the old church building – which we could build out to be their home theater space with seating for 233 guests, more than twice the size of their former black box theater.  We also offered them a very low rent rate for the new theater since the cost of renovation of the church was mostly being covered by FEMA and other grants.  Even with these incentives, it took some convincing on my part for the EWP Board to decide to become an anchor tenant in the building when the construction was finished.  I will always be grateful to Tom Donaldson, an EWP volunteer, who helped to convince the EWP board to finally decide to join the Union church project.",
          "text": "Meanwhile, EWP, after searching various sites, was not able to secure an alternate location for a theater in Hollywood.  I suggested to them to re-consider moving into the old church building – which we could build out to be their home theater space with seating for 233 guests, more than twice the size of their former black box theater.  We also offered them a very low rent rate for the new theater since the cost of renovation of the church was mostly being covered by FEMA and other grants.  Even with these incentives, it took some convincing on my part for the EWP Board to decide to become an anchor tenant in the building when the construction was finished.  I will always be grateful to Tom Donaldson, an EWP volunteer, who helped to convince the EWP board to finally decide to join the Union church project."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "html": "In February of 1998 the renovation construction was completed, due to the competent oversight of LTSC staff Lisa Sugino and Union Church project manager Erich Nakano.  The project came in at a cost of $4 million, and our project partner Visual Communication, and tenants LA Artcore, and East West Players moved in to what I decided should be called “The Union Center for the Arts”, connecting the new building with the name of the historic former church.  The building, once dead and deserted, has been resurrected to new life with three visual/cultural arts groups who came together to occupy this historic sacred space.  Not only has the building come back to life, but the Union Center for the Arts (UCA) has made an impact to increase the economic vitality of the neighborhood by attracting its many patrons to attend their programs and then also to support the local restaurants, gift shops, and museums.",
          "text": "In February of 1998 the renovation construction was completed, due to the competent oversight of LTSC staff Lisa Sugino and Union Church project manager Erich Nakano.  The project came in at a cost of $4 million, and our project partner Visual Communication, and tenants LA Artcore, and East West Players moved in to what I decided should be called “The Union Center for the Arts”, connecting the new building with the name of the historic former church.  The building, once dead and deserted, has been resurrected to new life with three visual/cultural arts groups who came together to occupy this historic sacred space.  Not only has the building come back to life, but the Union Center for the Arts (UCA) has made an impact to increase the economic vitality of the neighborhood by attracting its many patrons to attend their programs and then also to support the local restaurants, gift shops, and museums."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "html": "EWP opened its inaugural season at the UCA with a stirring production of “Pacific Overtures” by Stephen Sondheim, which drew large and enthusiastic crowds.  While watching this first performance, with Asian American actors singing and dancing on stage and the audience applauding in delight, I felt a bit like a Broadway show impresario only sans the tuxedo!  I had a sense of satisfaction of making a century-old building which had been left for dead to come back to life - not too shabby for a community organizer privileged to be engaged in a “real job”!",
          "text": "EWP opened its inaugural season at the UCA with a stirring production of “Pacific Overtures” by Stephen Sondheim, which drew large and enthusiastic crowds.  While watching this first performance, with Asian American actors singing and dancing on stage and the audience applauding in delight, I felt a bit like a Broadway show impresario only sans the tuxedo!  I had a sense of satisfaction of making a century-old building which had been left for dead to come back to life - not too shabby for a community organizer privileged to be engaged in a “real job”!"
        },
        {
          "type": "photo_callout",
          "photoNumber": "7",
          "image": "images/Photo#7.jpeg",
          "html": "See Photo#7:  The Union Center for the Arts as it stands today on Judge John Aiso Street, housing the East West Players Theater, Visual Communications, and LA Artcore.",
          "text": "See Photo#7:  The Union Center for the Arts as it stands today on Judge John Aiso Street, housing the East West Players Theater, Visual Communications, and LA Artcore."
        }
