          "html": "<p><em><strong>SIDEBAR</strong></em><em>:  </em><em>Bronzeville</em><em> came into being due to the racist “restrictive covenants” that existed in many municipalities and neighborhoods in Los Angeles.  After the bombing of Pearl Harbor, there were many folks who came to LA from the south and mid-west to get</em><em> newly created</em><em> jobs</em><em> related to the war effort</em><em>.  Poor whites were escaping the Dust Bowl of the 1930’s and Black folks were escaping harsh conditions of the segregated Jim Crow South</em><em>; large numbers migrated</em><em> to LA for the many new jobs that were cre</em><em>ated</em><em>.  LA was a hot-bed for work on aircraft and munitions and ship-building – however, for the many Blacks who came here to find work, they could not live in many places due to the existence of restrictive covenants aimed at Black people.  These racist covenants basically s</em><em>tate</em><em>d “if you are Black – go away, you can’t live here!”  However, Little Tokyo was not a restricted neighborhood and was now mostly empty of the thousands of former residents taken away to be incarcerated in remote camps; practically over-night, Little Tokyo changed from a Japanese American neighborhood to an African American neighborhood, which was then re-named “</em><em>Bronzeville</em><em>”</em></p>",
          "text": "SIDEBAR:  Bronzeville came into being due to the racist “restrictive covenants” that existed in many municipalities and neighborhoods in Los Angeles.  After the bombing of Pearl Harbor, there were many folks who came to LA from the south and mid-west to get newly created jobs related to the war effort.  Poor whites were escaping the Dust Bowl of the 1930’s and Black folks were escaping harsh conditions of the segregated Jim Crow South; large numbers migrated to LA for the many new jobs that were created.  LA was a hot-bed for work on aircraft and munitions and ship-building – however, for the many Blacks who came here to find work, they could not live in many places due to the existence of restrictive covenants aimed at Black people.  These racist covenants basically stated “if you are Black – go away, you can’t live here!”  However, Little Tokyo was not a restricted neighborhood and was now mostly empty of the thousands of former residents taken away to be incarcerated in remote camps; practically over-night, Little Tokyo changed from a Japanese American neighborhood to an African American neighborhood, which was then re-named “Bronzeville”"
